{"name":"Inferno","hex":"#da5736","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"inferno","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A place or situation resembling Hell.","A large fire; a conflagration."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/inferno"}],"entries":[{"title":"Hell","blocks":[{"text":"In religion and folklore, Hell is a location or state in the afterlife in which souls are subjected to punishment after death. Religions with a linear divine history sometimes depict hells as eternal, such as in some versions of Christianity and Islam, whereas religions with reincarnation usually depict a hell as an intermediary period between incarnations, as is the case in the Indian religions. Religions typically locate Hell in another dimension or under Earth's surface. Other afterlife destinations include heaven, paradise, purgatory, limbo, and the underworld."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Fra_Angelico_010.jpg/330px-Fra_Angelico_010.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hell","wikidata":"Q564"},{"title":"Conflagration","blocks":[{"text":"A conflagration is a particularly large and destructive fire. In the environment, this may describe a fire that spreads via structure to structure, ignition due to radiant or convective heat, or ember transmission. Conflagrations often damage human life, animal life, health, and/or property. A conflagration can begin accidentally or be intentionally created (arson). A very large fire can produce a firestorm, in which the central column of rising heated air induces strong inward winds, which supply oxygen to the fire. Conflagrations can cause casualties including deaths or injuries from burns, collapse of structures and attempts to escape, and smoke inhalation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Doe_Fire_looking_south_from_forest_highway_7_%282020-08-19%29.jpg/330px-Doe_Fire_looking_south_from_forest_highway_7_%282020-08-19%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conflagration","wikidata":"Q168983"},{"title":"Inferno (Dante)","blocks":[{"text":"Inferno is the first part of the Italian writer Dante Alighieri's 14th-century narrative poem the Divine Comedy, followed by Purgatorio and Paradiso. The Inferno describes the journey of a fictionalised version of Dante himself through Hell, guided by the ancient Roman poet Virgil. In the poem, Hell is depicted as nine concentric circles of torment located within the Earth; it is the \"realm [...] of those who have rejected spiritual values by yielding to bestial appetites or violence, or by perverting their human intellect to fraud or malice against their fellowmen\". As an allegory, the Divine Comedy represents the journey of the soul toward God, with the Inferno describing the recognition and rejection of sin."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/Inferno.jpg/330px-Inferno.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inferno_(Dante)","wikidata":"Q4509219"},{"title":"Inferno (2016 film)","blocks":[{"text":"Inferno is a 2016 American action mystery thriller film directed by Ron Howard and written by David Koepp. It is loosely based on the 2013 novel by Dan Brown. The sequel to The Da Vinci Code (2006) and Angels & Demons (2009), it is the third and final film in the Robert Langdon film series. It stars Tom Hanks, reprising his role as Robert Langdon, alongside Felicity Jones as Dr. Sienna Brooks, Omar Sy, Sidse Babett Knudsen, Ben Foster, and Irrfan Khan."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/6/66/Inferno_%282016_film%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inferno_(2016_film)","wikidata":"Q18192306"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/Hel_%281889%29_by_Johannes_Gehrts.jpg/500px-Hel_%281889%29_by_Johannes_Gehrts.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/Hel_%281889%29_by_Johannes_Gehrts.jpg/500px-Hel_%281889%29_by_Johannes_Gehrts.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Hel (1889) by Johannes Gehrts, depicts the Old Norse Hel, a goddess-like figure, in the location of the same name, which she oversees","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHel_(1889)_by_Johannes_Gehrts.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/Fresque_%C3%A9glise_huaro.JPG/500px-Fresque_%C3%A9glise_huaro.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/Fresque_%C3%A9glise_huaro.JPG/1280px-Fresque_%C3%A9glise_huaro.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/Fresque_%C3%A9glise_huaro.JPG/500px-Fresque_%C3%A9glise_huaro.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Preserved colonial wall paintings of 1802 depicting Hell,[14][15][16] by Tadeo Escalante, inside the Church of San Juan Bautista in Huaro, Peru","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFresque_%C3%A9glise_huaro.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/Dumuzi_aux_enfers.jpg/500px-Dumuzi_aux_enfers.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/Dumuzi_aux_enfers.jpg/1280px-Dumuzi_aux_enfers.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/Dumuzi_aux_enfers.jpg/500px-Dumuzi_aux_enfers.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.or